    <briefdescription>
<para>Functions used to compose logical expressions in tests. </para>
    </briefdescription>
    <detaileddescription>
<para>Three functions combine the conditions of a check, and report the values of all their operands when the check fails:<itemizedlist>
<listitem><para><computeroutput>mt::all_of (a, b, ...)</computeroutput> is true when all the conditions are true;</para>
</listitem><listitem><para><computeroutput>mt::any_of (a, b, ...)</computeroutput> is true when at least one condition is true, including when all are;</para>
</listitem><listitem><para><computeroutput>mt::negate (a)</computeroutput> is true when the condition is false.</para>
</listitem></itemizedlist>
</para>
<para><computeroutput>all_of()</computeroutput> and <computeroutput>any_of()</computeroutput> take two or more operands; a failed check shows them as one group, for example <computeroutput>(1 == 1 and 1 == 1 and 1 == 3)</computeroutput>.</para>
<para>The functions have names, rather than the <computeroutput>&amp;&amp;</computeroutput>, <computeroutput>||</computeroutput>, and <computeroutput>!</computeroutput> operators, because the built-in operators would return a plain <computeroutput>bool</computeroutput>, which loses the values of the operands.</para>
<para><simplesect kind="par"><title>Example</title><para></para>
</simplesect>
<programlisting filename=".cpp"><codeline><highlight class="normal">ts.test<sp/>(</highlight><highlight class="stringliteral">&quot;Check<sp/>compound<sp/>conditions&quot;</highlight><highlight class="normal">,<sp/>[]<sp/>(</highlight><highlight class="keyword">auto</highlight><highlight class="normal">&amp;<sp/>t)<sp/>{</highlight></codeline>
<codeline><highlight class="normal"><sp/><sp/>t.expect<sp/>(mt::all_of<sp/>(mt::eq<sp/>(compute_answer<sp/>(),<sp/>42),</highlight></codeline>
<codeline><highlight class="normal"><sp/><sp/><sp/><sp/><sp/><sp/><sp/><sp/><sp/><sp/><sp/><sp/><sp/><sp/><sp/><sp/><sp/><sp/><sp/><sp/><sp/><sp/><sp/><sp/>mt::eq<sp/>(compute_float<sp/>(),<sp/>42.0),</highlight></codeline>
<codeline><highlight class="normal"><sp/><sp/><sp/><sp/><sp/><sp/><sp/><sp/><sp/><sp/><sp/><sp/><sp/><sp/><sp/><sp/><sp/><sp/><sp/><sp/><sp/><sp/><sp/><sp/>mt::lt<sp/>(compute_answer<sp/>(),<sp/>43)));</highlight></codeline>
<codeline><highlight class="normal">});</highlight></codeline>
</programlisting></para>
<para><simplesect kind="par"><title>Differences from the built-in operators</title><para><itemizedlist>
<listitem><para>All the operands are evaluated before the check; unlike <computeroutput>&amp;&amp;</computeroutput> and <computeroutput>||</computeroutput>, the functions do not stop at the first operand that decides the result, so do not write <computeroutput>mt::all_of (mt::ne (p, nullptr), mt::eq (*p, 1))</computeroutput>.</para>
</listitem><listitem><para>After a <computeroutput>using namespace std</computeroutput>, an unqualified <computeroutput>all_of()</computeroutput> or <computeroutput>any_of()</computeroutput> with three or more operands of the same type selects the standard algorithm, which does not compile; write <computeroutput>mt::all_of()</computeroutput> and <computeroutput>mt::any_of()</computeroutput>.</para>
</listitem></itemizedlist>
</para>
</simplesect>
The overloaded logical operators of the <computeroutput>mt::operators</computeroutput> namespace are deprecated since 6.0.0, in favour of these functions.  </para>
    </detaileddescription>
